  data:  # 92ec
    kind: work_item  # 92ec
    title: "tracker-sync PRs conflict on GitHub because merge=union is client-side only; five branches sit unmerged since Aug 24 and all five merge CLEAN locally"  # 92ec
    status: todo_hard  # 92ec
    priority: 2  # 92ec
    parent:  # 92ec
    tags:  # 92ec
    - tracker  # 92ec
    - infrastructure  # 92ec
    isbefore: []  # 92ec
    duplicate_of: []  # 92ec
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 92ec
    pr_ref:  # 92ec
    description: "DIAGNOSED 2026-09-02 from the owner's report that tracker-sync PRs #682/#683/#706\nshow conflicts. They are not corrupt and the conflicts are not real: EVERY\nunmerged tracker-sync branch merges CLEANLY on a client.\n\nMEASURED, read-only, against origin/dev at 4ef3b42e54, using `git merge-tree\n--write-tree` (which honors .gitattributes):\n\n    tracker-sync/20260824-160016   1 unmerged commit   CLEAN\n    tracker-sync/20260830-015453   1                   CLEAN\n    tracker-sync/20260901-014222   1                   CLEAN\n    tracker-sync/20260901-014517   1                   CLEAN\n    tracker-sync/20260902-015521   1                   CLEAN\n\nFive branches, all clean, the oldest from AUGUST 24.\n\nTHE CAUSE. .gitattributes declares, correctly and deliberately:\n\n    .agent/tracker/.ops/.*.ops             linguist-generated  merge=union  diff=tracker\n    .agent/tracker-workspace/.ops/.*.ops   linguist-generated  merge=union  diff=tracker\n\n`merge=union` is what makes an append-only op journal conflict-free: on overlap,\nkeep both sides' lines. It is a CLIENT-SIDE mechanism. GitHub's server-side merge\ndoes not read .gitattributes and does not apply union (or any other) merge\ndriver — it performs a plain three-way text merge. So two branches that appended\nto the same .ops file are a textual conflict on the server and a trivial union on\nany client.\n\nWHY THIS IS STRUCTURAL AND NOT A BACKLOG. Retrying does not help and never will.\nAny two tracker-sync PRs open at once that touch the same .ops file WILL conflict\nserver-side, by construction. The queue grows monotonically because the sync\nmechanism creates the overlap it cannot then resolve: each new sync branches from\ndev and appends to the same journals the previous unmerged branch appended to.\n\nOPTIONS, none built, ordered by how structural they are.\n  1. AUTO-SYNC REBASES BEFORE PUSHING. A rebase runs on the client, so union\n     applies; the PR then fast-forwards and the server never merges anything.\n     This is the fix that matches the file format's own design. Auto-sync already\n     has rebase machinery (auto-pr's rebase path copies .ops dirs around a\n     rebase), so the shape exists.\n  2. AUTO-SYNC MERGES INTO ONE BRANCH rather than opening a new one per cycle\n     while a previous one is unmerged. Smaller change, leaves the server-merge\n     hazard in place for the single branch.\n  3. Merge the five by hand on a client and push. Unblocks today, fixes nothing;\n     dev is protected so it needs whoever holds bypass.\n\nNOT A GOVERNANCE FILE, worth stating because it looks like one. The .ops DATA\nlives under .agent/**, but the auto-sync CODE lives in packages/hypergumbo-tracker/,\nwhich is not on AGENTS.md's governance list. Option 1 is implementable without a\ngovernance exception; MERGING the PRs is still the owner's, since those PRs touch\n.agent/**.\n\nDO NOT \"FIX\" THIS BY DROPPING merge=union. The union driver is what makes the op\njournal safe; removing it to satisfy a server that ignores it would trade a\ncosmetic conflict for real data loss."  # 92ec
    fields: {}  # 92ec
