  message: "OWNER RULING 2026-09-02: P2 -> P1. 'This is high priority because the tracker is the backbone of much of our development work.' Taking it ahead of WI-vosug; the WI-vosug escape-site measurement continues in the background since it reads hypergumbo_core and cannot be contaminated by tracker edits."  # c417
- op: discuss  # df50
  at: '2026-09-02T17:08:07Z'  # df50
  by: agent  # df50
  actor: jgstern_agent  # df50
  clock: 4  # df50
  nonce: df50  # df50
  message: "CORRECTION TO THIS ITEM'S OWN RECOMMENDATION. The MECHANISM as filed is confirmed; the CONSEQUENCE I drew from it was wrong, and the fix is far smaller than option 1.\n\nWHAT THE OWNER'S DATUM FORCED. 'It was working a while on GitHub, things started piling up these past 2-3 days.' That is inconsistent with 'GitHub never honored merge=union' being a NEW problem, so the mechanism was re-tested rather than defended.\n\nMECHANISM, CONFIRMED on .INV-juvul-*.ops for tracker-sync/20260824-160016:\n    merge-base (dev when branch was cut)   32 lines\n    branch tip                             54 lines  (+22 appended)\n    current dev                            61 lines  (+29 DIFFERENT lines appended)\nBoth sides appended at the END of one append-only file from a common base.\nmerge=union resolves that trivially client-side; GitHub's plain three-way sees\ntwo different additions at one location and calls it a conflict. So nothing broke\nrecently: while syncs merged PROMPTLY, dev never diverged on those files before\nthe PR landed. The moment one PR sits unmerged and any other sync appends to the\nsame file, it becomes PERMANENTLY unmergeable server-side. The Sept 1-2 axis\ncampaign (WI-kurod/WI-kijup/WI-mubup) plus INV-foluz drove heavy tracker\nthroughput, which is why the window slammed shut now rather than earlier.\n\n*** THE PART I GOT WRONG, AND IT REVERSES THE ACTION. *** This item said the five\nbranches need merging and proposed rebasing auto-sync (option 1). Measured\ninstead, two independent ways:\n\n  comm -23 over every file each branch touches, branch vs current dev:\n      20260824-160016   0 lines not already in dev\n      20260830-015453   0\n      20260901-014222   0\n      20260901-014517   0\n      20260902-015521   0\n\n  git merge-tree --write-tree <branch> vs origin/dev^{tree}:\n      ALL FIVE produce a tree IDENTICAL to dev's current tree (1b702f249a).\n\nThey are COMPLETE NO-OPS. Every op they carry is already in dev.\n\nWHY, AND IT IS THE GOOD NEWS: THE DURABILITY GUARANTEE IS INTACT. When a sync\nfails to merge, its ops stay in the local working tree (WI-lufal gates the\ndestructive cleanup on merge success), so the NEXT sync rebuilds from a fresh\norigin/dev base and carries them forward. The DATA always gets through. What is\nleft behind is litter, not stranded ops.\n\nACTION: CLOSE ALL FIVE PRs. Do not merge them, do not rebase anything, do not\ntouch merge=union. There is nothing to recover and no code change is needed for\nthe repair.\n\nRE-SCOPED REMEDY, much smaller than options 1-3 as filed. Auto-sync does not\nclean up its own superseded branches. A prune step -- for each open\ntracker-sync/* PR, if  equals\norigin/dev's tree, the branch is superseded and its PR can be closed -- is the\nwhole fix. That predicate is exactly the no-op test used above, it is decidable\nlocally with no API guessing, and it cannot close a branch carrying unique ops.\n\nOPTION 1 (rebase before push) IS WITHDRAWN as unnecessary: do_sync already builds\neach sync commit on a freshly fetched origin/dev via plumbing (read-tree from\nbase_sha), so a NEW sync is never stale. Only ABANDONED branches go stale, and\nclosing them is the answer to that."  # df50
- op: discuss  # d6da
